{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794979102,"nonce":"jDdspB96ug8F7rZOS7lidRCMzpA=","message":"Native recorded-input validation completed on 2026-11-18 after the owner unlocked the Mac. Checkpoint remains 8576bc09090cbb2a0492625a082ab867f521e551. No new production code or build was used during these measurements. Issue remains OPEN.\n\nThe replay now works through the actual Cocoa/winit/desktop input path. The shadow-free window capture establishes 2x backing scale; deltas are converted accordingly. It posts only while the verified owned probe is foreground and the session is unlocked. Live foreground queries avoid AppKit's cached foreground result. The 3748 recorded application-delivered events take 51.972 seconds, including 10.967 seconds of vertical setup and approximately 41 seconds of rapid continuous one-direction LEFT Calendar swipes. This is application-delivery timing/rounded coordinates, not original raw HID. macOS coalesces adjacent movement events.\n\nSeparate traced calibration delivered 3460 events from the 3748 source events, with all 388 Started boundaries preserved. A stale leading Ended repeated dy=-2 because the preceding four-event calibration left the OS gesture open. Excluding only that documented boundary event makes every position's accumulated X/Y deltas and the 388 Ended boundaries exact. The driver now resets the old OS gesture with a zero Ended before starting a recording. Screenshots confirm left Calendar advances from November 2026 to September 2039, while the right Calendar remains November 2026. This replaces the earlier locked/failed-delivery attempts; those attempts are not benchmark evidence.\n\nControlled native A/B: baseline dd1dad57 vs candidate 2bfd9ead, fresh process/default date/900x900 content in the same app bundle, CPU rows, motion promotion off, RUST_LOG=warn, motion tracing unset. Alternating baseline/candidate/baseline/candidate. No Cargo build, debugger sampler or input/frame tracing ran during the four comparisons. Starting window screenshots are byte-identical; all four end on the same left/right months. One settled screenshot differs by a few pixels of scroll offset, so this is not a native RGBA-equality claim.\n\nCPU is total process CPU, expressed as percentage of one core. proc_pid_rusage counters are converted from hardware ticks using mach_timebase_info (125/3 ns per tick), cross-checked against ps. Samples approximately every 250 ms; rolling one-second peaks use four intervals. Report the windows explicitly rather than comparing these directly to Activity Monitor's averaging window.\n\nTrial 1 baseline: active average 70.410%, 1s peak 88.960%, 1s p95 85.652%, 250ms peak 96.645%.\nTrial 2 candidate: active average 61.714%, 1s peak 77.061%, 1s p95 74.397%, 250ms peak 85.222%.\nTrial 3 baseline: active average 69.589%, 1s peak 88.656%, 1s p95 84.526%, 250ms peak 104.545%.\nTrial 4 candidate: active average 62.879%, 1s peak 82.204%, 1s p95 77.356%, 250ms peak 90.261%.\n\nThe baseline reproduces the reported high load. Candidate mean CPU is about 62.30% vs 70.00% baseline (11.00% relative reduction). One-second peaks improve, but the 85-90% shorter bursts remain: this is a measured improvement, not resolution of the high-CPU/pause issue. Maximum send lateness was 2.38-5.22 ms. No user had to repeat timed manual swipes.\n\nMemory: active RSS peaks baseline 140.45/143.16 MiB vs candidate 142.59/145.31 MiB. Physical footprint peaks baseline 257.24/233.64 MiB vs candidate 228.08/231.58 MiB. No active pageins in these processes. These mixed/two-repeat observations do not establish a controlled RSS/memory-pressure improvement or a leak.\n\nA SEPARATE diagnostic replay on the candidate sampled native stacks for 40 seconds at 5ms, beginning 11.052 seconds into the recording, excluding initial navigation. Sample succeeded with 6774 main-thread samples. Substantial application leaves remain in RandomState/SipHash LayoutNodeId lookups, draws_outside_at, moves_its_content/is_layout_animating, get_absolute_bounds, subtree_paints_over_at/later_content_overlaps_with, and paint/content walks. This directs the next investigation toward repeated culling/order traversal and animation metadata lookup work. It does not prove that Calendar root rebuilding or presentation waits cause the pause. The sample is aggregate; an owner-observed pause was not timestamp-correlated in this run, so pause resolution remains unverified.\n\nEvidence: target/calendar-probe/2026-11-18-native-replay/{calibration/delivery-validation.json,native-ab/{comparison.json,summary.json,visual-validation.json,1-baseline,2-candidate,3-baseline,4-candidate},candidate-profile/{report.json,analysis.json,native.sample.txt,native.demangled.txt},scripts,script-sha256.json}. Raw counters, exact replay reports, logs and owned-window screenshots are retained. Preserved native binaries and recorded-input source are unchanged. Candidate owned PID20447 remains open with tracing off, no active sampling/replay; launcher session58467 waits for the app.\n\nNext: reduce the remaining repeated traversal/hash work against this reproducible native workload, retain the focused paint/culling/Calendar pixel gates, and capture time-correlated frame/input data around an actual pause before attributing its cause. No FPS cap or animation-fidelity reduction. No source/tracker push or issue closure.","files":null}]}